## 2. THE RESOLUTION OWNER — `NameBinding`

**Responsibility:** bind a written name to the symbol it binds to and the type it denotes, recovering the type from the node's type information when binding fails.

```csharp
// analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/NameBinding.cs
internal static class NameBinding
{
    internal static (ISymbol? Symbol, ITypeSymbol? Type) Resolve(
        SemanticModel semanticModel, NameSyntax name, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        ISymbol? symbol = semanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(name, cancellationToken).Symbol;
        if (symbol is not null)
        {
            return (symbol, NonError(Denoted(symbol)));
        }

        return (null, NonError(semanticModel.GetTypeInfo(name, cancellationToken).Type));
    }

    private static ITypeSymbol? Denoted(ISymbol symbol) => symbol switch
    {
        ITypeSymbol type => type,
        IMethodSymbol { MethodKind: MethodKind.Constructor } constructor => constructor.ContainingType,
        _ => null,
    };

    private static ITypeSymbol? NonError(ITypeSymbol? type) =>
        type is null || type.TypeKind == TypeKind.Error ? null : type;
}
```

`NameSyntax` is the parameter type because it is the tightest supertype both callers already hold: `WrittenNameScanner` passes a `SimpleNameSyntax` (IdentifierName/GenericName), `AttributeIdentity` passes `attribute.Name`.

**What `NameBinding` owns — measured as common to both callers:** the `GetSymbolInfo` bind (a, b); the constructor → containing-type mapping (a — the attribute's name binds to the constructor in *both* callers); the `GetTypeInfo` recovery gated on nothing having bound (b, c); the error-type rejection on both tiers (c).

**What stays in `AttributeIdentity` — only it needs these:** the `…Attribute` suffix normalisation and the syntactic simple-name fallback `MatchesSyntactically` (`AttributeIdentity.cs:77-90`), which the general lens must NOT inherit — a general lens falling back to a simple name would report an unrelated same-named type in another namespace; the candidate-identity match through `WellKnownType.IsAnyOf` (`WellKnownType.cs:38`); narrowing the denoted type to `INamedTypeSymbol` (only it can, per (d)).

**What stays in `WrittenNameScanner` — only it needs these:** the two leaf `SyntaxKind`s, the `RegisterSyntaxNodeAction`, the per-assembly gate, the documentation-reference exclusion (a cref cannot occur in an attribute name), and the namespace-symbol drop (f).

**What is deliberately NOT in the helper:** an `IAliasSymbol` arm (e, dead); a `SyntaxKind` list or any registration (attributes register through `SyntaxKind.Attribute` in AG0008/AG0105 and never through the two leaf kinds); the cref test.

### Resulting `AttributeIdentity`

The whole body of `ResolveAttributeType` (`AttributeIdentity.cs:60-72`) was the two tiers, so it becomes a one-line pass-through:

```csharp
private static INamedTypeSymbol? ResolveAttributeType(
    SemanticModel semanticModel, AttributeSyntax attribute, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    return NameBinding.Resolve(semanticModel, attribute.Name, cancellationToken).Type as INamedTypeSymbol;
}
```

A one-line pass-through with no behaviour of its own is a second name for the owner, so the pick is to fold it into the one caller and delete the method:

```csharp
internal static bool IsAnyOf(
    SemanticModel semanticModel,
    AttributeSyntax attribute,
    ImmutableArray<(string Namespace, string Name)> candidates,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    if (NameBinding.Resolve(semanticModel, attribute.Name, cancellationToken).Type is INamedTypeSymbol resolved)
    {
        return WellKnownType.IsAnyOf(resolved, candidates);
    }

    return MatchesSyntactically(attribute, candidates);
}
```

`MatchesSyntactically` and the class doc comment's account of the syntactic fallback stay; the doc comment's account of the two tiers moves to `NameBinding`. AG0008 and AG0105 are behaviourally unchanged, proved by their existing tests staying green with no edit.

### Resulting `WrittenNameScanner`

```csharp
// analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/WrittenNameScanner.cs
internal static class WrittenNameScanner
{
    internal static readonly ImmutableArray<SyntaxKind> NameKinds =
        ImmutableArray.Create(SyntaxKind.IdentifierName, SyntaxKind.GenericName);

    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol?, ITypeSymbol?> inspect);

    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol?, ITypeSymbol?> inspect);

    internal static (ISymbol? Symbol, ITypeSymbol? Type) Resolve(
        SemanticModel semanticModel, SimpleNameSyntax name, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        if (IsDocumentationReference(name))
        {
            return default;
        }

        (ISymbol? symbol, ITypeSymbol? type) = NameBinding.Resolve(semanticModel, name, cancellationToken);
        return symbol is INamespaceSymbol ? default : (symbol, type);
    }

    internal static bool IsDocumentationReference(SyntaxNode node) =>
        node.FirstAncestorOrSelf<CrefSyntax>() is not null;
}
```

`Resolve` is the single path: the registered dispatch calls it, and the direct scanner tests call it, so the exclusion cannot be applied in one and not the other. Dispatch fires `inspect` when `Symbol is not null || Type is not null`. `RegisterForAssembly` gates through `OwnerClass.InAssembly` (`OwnerClass.cs:107`), the existing owner of the compilation-assembly-name comparison — not a third copy of the inline comparison at `MemberUseScanner.cs:67`.

Two kinds, not an enumeration of positions: a generic type written with arguments is ONE `GenericName` node whose identifier is a token, so dropping `GenericName` leaves no other node; and because the set is not an enumeration of syntax positions it is structurally incapable of AG0007's failure mode (`ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:49-52`, four hand-picked kinds).

