## 3. THE DOCUMENTATION-REFERENCE EXCLUSION

**Discriminator: `node.FirstAncestorOrSelf<CrefSyntax>() is not null`.**

Measured against the three candidates over one fixture carrying every cref form, every preprocessor directive kind, both doc-comment forms, and ordinary code:

| node | parent kind | `IsPartOfStructuredTrivia` | `DocumentationCommentTriviaSyntax` ancestor | `CrefSyntax` ancestor |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `Guarded` in `<see cref="Guarded"/>` | `NameMemberCref` | True | True | True |
| `Guarded` in `<see cref="Guarded.Member"/>` | `QualifiedCref` | True | True | True |
| `Guarded` in `<see cref="Cache{Guarded}"/>` | **`TypeArgumentList`** | True | True | True |
| `Guarded` in `<see cref="Guarded.op_Addition(Guarded, Guarded)"/>` | **`CrefParameter`** | True | True | True |
| `Cache{T}` in `<see cref="Cache{T}"/>` | `NameMemberCref` | True | True | True |
| `Guarded` in `/** <see cref="Guarded"/> */` | `NameMemberCref` | True | True | True |
| `TItem` in `<typeparam name="TItem">` | `XmlNameAttribute` | True | True | **False** |
| `p` in `<param name="p">` | `XmlNameAttribute` | True | True | **False** |
| `GUARDED` in `#if GUARDED` | `IfDirectiveTrivia` | **True** | False | False |
| `CA1822` in `#pragma warning disable CA1822` | `PragmaWarningDirectiveTrivia` | **True** | False | False |
| every ordinary code position (field/property/method/parameter/local declaration, `typeof`, `nameof`, cast, `as`, `is`, `case`, base list, constraint, generic argument, array element, object creation, member access, using directive, using alias, `using static`, `global::`) | — | **False** | **False** | **False** |

**Why the parent-kind enumeration is rejected:** it leaks. `Guarded` inside `Cache{Guarded}` has parent `TypeArgumentList` and `Guarded` inside a cref parameter list has parent `CrefParameter` — neither is a cref kind, so `{NameMemberCref, QualifiedCref}` lets both through.

**Why `IsPartOfStructuredTrivia` is rejected:** it is True for preprocessor-directive names as well. Their symbols are null today so there is no behavioural difference, but the test would then be excluding a construct it does not name, and one `#pragma`/`#if` identifier that ever binds changes the rule's meaning without anyone editing it.

**Why `CrefSyntax` and not `DocumentationCommentTriviaSyntax`:** every node with a `CrefSyntax` ancestor also has a `DocumentationCommentTriviaSyntax` ancestor (columns 3 and 4 above), and `CrefSyntax` nodes occur only inside `XmlCrefAttributeSyntax` inside a documentation comment — so the cref test cannot exclude anything outside a doc comment. It is the narrower of the two by exactly the `<param name>` / `<typeparam name>` / `<typeparamref name>` rows, which bind to a parameter or a type parameter of the documented member and can never denote a guarded type. The settled requirement is "XML documentation **references**"; a cref is that, and nothing else is.

**Non-documentation constructs the choice was checked against and does not exclude:** field, property, method-return, method-parameter and local declarations; inferred `var`; `typeof`; `nameof` bare and dotted; cast; `as`; `is` pattern; `case` pattern; generic type argument; generic constraint; base list; interface list; operator declaration; array element; object creation; member access; invocation receiver; `using` directive; `using` alias declaration and use; `using static` directive leaf; `global::`-qualified name; `#define`; `#if`; `#region`; `#pragma warning`; `#nullable`; `#line`; and a plain `//` comment (not a node at all).

**What the exclusion costs.** Inside the gated compilations a documentation comment may name any guarded type or member freely, from any method including a decoy, with no diagnostic — AG0041's Engine internals in `guard` and `AgentGuard.TestHelpers`, AG0023's CrossPlatform-core types and AG0029's per-OS types in Engine. The rules no longer prove that documentation stays off a forbidden path. What it does not cost: a cref produces no operation (i), so no *use* can hide behind one; the compiler still requires the cref to bind (CS1574, an error under `TreatWarningsAsErrors`), so it cannot cover a typo; and AG0040, AG0006 and AG0007 are untouched because none of them uses the written-name lens.

**The three live links, preserved unchanged.** `src/AgentGuard.Cli/Program.cs:17` and `:53`, each `<see cref="SystemServices.Create"/>` in the `guard` compilation; `src/AgentGuard.Boundaries/SystemServices.cs:79`, `<see cref="CrossPlatformAdapters"/>` inside `Create()`'s own doc comment, which travels with the relocated file into Engine. Measured end to end: the AG0041-shaped lens drops from 8 to 6 diagnostics over the CLI fixture, the two removed being exactly the two cref nodes, while `typeof(SystemServices)`, `nameof(SystemServices)` and the other-internal member use stay reported and `SystemServices.Create()` stays accepted; the AG0023-shaped Engine gate drops from 3 to 2, the one removed being exactly the cref inside `Create()`'s doc comment. The other crefs in `SystemServices.cs` (`:10`, `:14`, `:24`) name `ISystemServices` (Abstractions, in no rule's type set) and `Create` (Engine's own, and AG0041 does not gate Engine) and were already clean.

